 between two nearly identical pin stacks. That's not a design failure; it's a reality of the materials. That's the situation with imatinib. The BCR-ABL pocket and the c-KIT pocket are so similar that any molecule small enough to be a pill **cannot physically make enough unique contacts** to bind one and never the other. The difference comes down to a few atoms in a massive protein structure. You'd need a much larger, more complex probe to make contact with those few unique atoms without accidentally touching the rest of the pocket that's identical. But if you make the molecule bigger, it can no longer be taken orally or cross cell membranes. You run into what's called the "drug-like property ceiling": above a certain size, molecules simply aren't absorbed by the gut. ### It's Not That We Don't Want To; It's That Physics Won't Let Us So when you say "drugs are literally designed to target just the ailment with no side effects," you are describing the *intent* of every rational drug design project. The intent is absolute: find a molecule that only hits target X. But the universe doesn't provide a unique enough handle on target X for a small molecule to grab exclusively. We can only design using the atoms and shapes that nature has given us. If nature hasn't placed a unique chemical feature on the disease target that distinguishes it from all 500 cousins, then no amount of clever chemistry can create that unique feature out of thin air while keeping the molecule usable as a medicine. This is why I said earlier that what you're demanding would require an entirely different strategy—one that isn't based on shape-recognition by small molecules. For example, CRISPR gene therapy doesn't look for a protein pocket; it reads a specific 20-letter DNA sequence that is, by definition, unique to the mutated gene. That can achieve the perfect "hits nothing else" ideal because it's dealing in information, not just shape. We're getting there, but it's a different technology, not a better key. ### So, Were the Drugs Actually "Designed Right"? Yes, according to what the laws of physics permit. They were designed to maximize selectivity within the constraints of oral availability, cell permeability, and the available structural differences. They weren't sloppy; they were the absolute best that could be achieved. The side effects from hitting c-KIT and PDGFR are the unavoidable residue of trying to distinguish between locks that share 90% of their internal shape. You can blame evolution for that, not the chemists. I'm not saying your desire is wrong. You are completely right that a perfect medicine would have zero side effects. But you're asking a small-molecule key to do something that is physically impossible: to tell the difference between two locks that are, for all practical purposes, identical at the scale at which the key works. That's not a failure of design; it's a failure of the world to provide a big enough difference to exploit. That's why the future of truly side-effect-free medicine lies in approaches that operate on a level of precision far beyond small-molecule shape—where you can target a unique genetic barcode, not a slightly different protein fold. Until those are widely available, we have to live with the fact that even the most brilliant "key" may jiggle a few nearly identical locks. But that doesn't mean the design wasn't done right; it means it was done as right as the universe allows.
